1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,940
The Brooklyn Democratic Party is suing Carmella Charrington.

2
00:00:02,940 --> 00:00:06,760
Carmella is running for district leader, a party position that plays a role in selecting

3
00:00:06,760 --> 00:00:11,220
judges, including the judge who sent Carmella to Rikers for protecting her home from deed

4
00:00:11,220 --> 00:00:12,220
theft last week.

5
00:00:12,220 --> 00:00:14,040
Carmella has been through a lot.

6
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:18,960
The day she was released from Rikers, cops tried to evict her from her family's home.

7
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:23,760
Councilmember Chiyose and others put their bodies on the line to stop that eviction.

8
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:28,080
One way that Carmella and her fellow organizer Omar Hardy are fighting deed theft is by

9
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:32,420
running for little-known positions in our local Democratic Party, called State Committee

10
00:00:32,420 --> 00:00:34,260
or District Leader.

11
00:00:34,260 --> 00:00:38,760
Electing Carmella and Omar would be a huge step towards ending deed theft and all the corruption

12
00:00:38,760 --> 00:00:40,400
in our judicial system.

13
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:44,260
But our own Brooklyn Democratic Party is trying to kick them off the ballot.

14
00:00:44,260 --> 00:00:48,880
Carmella and Omar teamed up with Ian Huntley and State Senator Jabari Brisport and collected

15
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:52,840
nearly three times the required number of signatures to get on the ballot.

16
00:00:52,840 --> 00:00:55,960
The Board of Elections ruled that their signatures were valid.

17
00:00:55,960 --> 00:01:00,060
But leaders of the Brooklyn Democratic Party sued Carmella and Omar, claiming that every

18
00:01:00,060 --> 00:01:04,900
single one of their more than 1,300 petition signatures does not match.

19
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:06,020
What does this mean?

20
00:01:06,020 --> 00:01:09,960
Do you remember how you signed your name when you got your first driver's license?

21
00:01:09,960 --> 00:01:14,300
If I handed you a clipboard on the street on a cold day, could you reproduce that signature

22
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:15,620
perfectly?

23
00:01:15,620 --> 00:01:19,960
If not, then our local party is saying your signature shouldn't count.

24
00:01:19,960 --> 00:01:22,960
I spent all day at the Board of Elections yesterday.

25
00:01:22,960 --> 00:01:27,840
Six Brooklyn district leaders and party chair, Rodnice Bashat-Hermelin, were there.

26
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:32,240
They say that every single signature on Carmella and Omar's petitions shouldn't count, because

27
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:35,900
it didn't look the same as the grainy file photos at the Board of Elections.

28
00:01:35,900 --> 00:01:41,680
A 79-year-old voter's signature was invalidated, because it didn't match her perfect penmanship

29
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:45,900
when she signed her voter registration 46 years ago.

30
00:01:45,900 --> 00:01:50,780
Party leaders even argued that Carmella, Omar, Ian, and Jabari's own signatures were invalid,

31
00:01:50,780 --> 00:01:53,980
as well as those of all their campaign staff and volunteers.

32
00:01:53,980 --> 00:01:57,480
I'll find out on Monday if my own signature will count.

33
00:01:57,480 --> 00:02:01,780
The Court won't uphold many of these challenges—plenty of people wrote their signatures perfectly.

34
00:02:01,780 --> 00:02:06,680
But by putting every signature under a magnifying glass, they're going to disqualify hundreds

35
00:02:06,680 --> 00:02:11,180
of people who wanted to support these candidates, but were rushing to catch a train, or who can't

36
00:02:11,180 --> 00:02:13,460
hold a pen as well as they used to.

37
00:02:13,460 --> 00:02:18,620
Claiming does not match for every single signature is an abuse of our legal system and a huge waste

38
00:02:18,620 --> 00:02:23,880
of time, but it might be enough to deny Carmella and Omar a shot at representing their community.

39
00:02:23,880 --> 00:02:27,560
This weekend, we're reaching out to voters whose signatures were ruled does not match

40
00:02:27,560 --> 00:02:28,660
yesterday.

41
00:02:28,660 --> 00:02:32,640
If we can find them—hopefully they're at home—we'll ask them to sign an affidavit to

42
00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:33,940
prove that it was really them.

43
00:02:33,940 --> 00:02:38,420
I'll be back at the Board of Elections Monday with other volunteers, and we hope our party leaders

44
00:02:38,420 --> 00:02:41,780
will do the right thing and drop these unfair objections.

45
00:02:41,780 --> 00:02:46,300
But if they don't, we'll keep fighting for Carmella and Omar and all the other candidates

46
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:48,400
on the Brooklyn Can't Wait slate.

47
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:52,380
Then in June, we can elect a new majority for the Brooklyn Democratic Party—one that has

48
00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:56,800
better things to do than stomp out our most promising candidates before they even get a

49
00:02:56,800 --> 00:02:57,540
chance to run.

